  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDeep upper troughing lingers across the Northeast through the \nweekend while expansive surface high pressure gradually slides east \nout of the Plains. The resultant setup for us locally will favor \nperiodic lake effect showers, most prevalent in the daytime \nhours, supported by rounds of vorticity lobes streaming through \nand lake to air temperature delta-T exceeding favorable \ndifference. Expect that low-level flow will back more on \nSaturday favoring a more northwesterly direction and thus the \nlake effect precipitation footprint shifts into western PA. \nCoverage will ramp up into the late morning hours as modest \ndaytime heating steepens low-level lapse rates and generates \nfumes of instability within the boundary layer. REFS members \nhave come into better agreement on the development of a Huron to\nErie connection band that will stream through Lawrence, Mercer,\nButler, and Venango Counties with the most intensity but \npotentially extend as far south as the ridges of Westmo. It does\nlook like a secondary concentrated area of showers could extend\nfrom Cleveland down into Columbiana County, and a tertiary area\ninto Forest County. Also expect that upslope snow will ramp up \nas well with more favorable elevation-orthogonal flow direction \nhoned in on Fayette, Westmoreland, and Indiana. It's actually \nquite plausible that the bulk of eastern OH, far southwestern \nPA, and northern WV remain predominantly dry on Saturday.\n\nAll that said, still expect that the dominant precipitation type\nfor most will be a cold rain with some graupel mixing in at \ntimes. For our I-80 corridor counties, REFS thermal profiles \nkeep temperatures just above freezing, and there may only be a \nnarrow window in which dew points sit just below freezing early \nSaturday morning whereas a thump of heavier precip could briefly\nwet-bulb temps to the near-freezing dews and allow for a mix in\nof wet snow. At this point, the subsidence inversion pokes just\ninto the bottom of the DGZ which would support some snow \ncrystal growth. Probability for a tenth of an inch of snow \nreaches about 50-60% along and north of I-80, save Forest County\nwhere probs are a bit higher and just a bit colder temps could \nallow for a couple tenths of an inch. Farther south, there's a \nshot that some wet flakes mix in, but have less confidence with \nsouthward extent owing to warmer surface temps and higher dews.\n\nThe aforementioned upslope snow in the PA ridges will persist \nSaturday into Sunday, though the bulk of any accumulation will occur \nin the overnight and early morning hours when it's coldest, with \nmelting likely during the day. Probability for >1\" of snow focuses \non the higher elevation of Westmo, Fayette, Preston, and Tucker at \n30-50%. Overall limited liquid-equivalent and lack of deep DGZ \nmoisture looks to lend a low chance (10-20%) of exceeding 2\".\n\nCome Sunday, 500 mb heights rise as the trough pushes off to the \neast and the surface high moves in closer lowering the subsidence \ninversion and cutting off the depth of the moisture into the \nafternoon hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nUpper troughing exits on Monday as the surface high eventually \nbecomes the dominant feature. Increasing subsidence and flow backing \nmore westerly will cut off the moisture fetch and mixing will \ngradually erode lingering stratocu coverage. Dry weather returns \nacross the board into at least the middle of next week. Daytime \nhighs will moderate back to above-normal values within weak \nsoutherly flow, but diurnal temperature swings will be notable as \nlight wind and clearing skies allow for efficient radiational \ncooling and a good probability of a couple consecutive nights of \nlows in the 20s.",
